  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...This afternoon, a wavy frontal boundary \nstretches west to east from the Ohio Valley to the Mid-Atlantic.\nSouth of the front, a moistening southerly flow is ongoing \nacross the Southeast U.S, with instability steadily building. \nCase in point, dewpoints today are about 5 degrees higher than \nyesterday across the board. There is still some residual dry air\naloft, but it continues to erode and the PWATs are bumping up \nagainst 2\" which is above the 90th percentile for this time of \nyear. \n\nFor the rest of today, residual subsidence beneath upper \nridging shifting offshore may hold convective coverage down \nsome. However, with moisture and instability increasing, we \nstill expect at least isolated convection to develop, primarily \nalong sea/river/sound breezes. Later this evening and tonight, \nthe above-mentioned cold front is forecast to dip into the \nnorthern counties of ENC, and this may support some continuation\nof convection in that area past sunset. Storm motions parallel \nto front may allow some training of convection, with a \nminor/nuisance-stayle flooding risk possible. With some residual\ndry air aloft, DCAPE is forecast to be around 1000j/kg, \nsupporting a risk of gusty downburst winds of 40-50 mph.\n\nEarly Saturday morning, convection may be ongoing in the \nvicinity of the stalled front. There may also be some convection\nalong the southern coastal areas thanks to an approaching \nshortwave and the diurnal land-breeze circulation. Through the \nday, PWATs continue to climb above 2\" for the entire area. The \nabove-mentioned shortwave is forecast to pass west of ENC, but \nshould provide enough of a glancing blow combined with the \nseabreeze and an inland trough to support scattered convection \ncapable of very heavy rainfall rates and gusty winds. The \nincreased southerly flow should lead to a more progressive \nseabreeze which should also favor a more progressive nature to \nthe convection. While this would limit the duration of heavy \nrainfall rates over any one location, the rates could be high \nenough to support minor hydro concerns, especially in urban \nareas. Of note, despite anomalous moisture and moderate \ninstability, convective coverage on Saturday is less certain as \nthe strongest forcing is expected to be outside of ENC. Despite \nmoderate instability, weak shear and lower DCAPE should help to \nkeep the severe thunderstorm risk at a minimum.\n\nOn Sunday, guidance continues the trend of slowing down the \narrival of a cold front that will be dropping south out of \nVirginia. The front now looks to come through Sunday night. This\npoints towards an extra day of moist and unstable conditions, \nwith another round of scattered to perhaps numerous \nthunderstorms. Deep layer shear is forecast to slightly \nincrease, but the overall severe thunderstorm risk continues to\nlook low. With anomalous PWATs in place, heavy rainfall rates \ncan be expected, with a continued risk of minor flooding \nimpacts.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A much drier airmass is forecast to build in \nacross the Eastern U.S. early next week in the wake of Sunday \nnight's cold front. Additionally, guidance have trended a bit \ncooler within the developing northeasterly flow regime, offering\na reprieve from the higher humidity over this weekend. From \nmid-week on, guidance differ on whether it warms up or stays \nmilder. Additionally, guidance suggests a decent coastal trough \nmay develop along what is leftover from this weekend's front. \nWith ridging building in, this trough may slide west beneath the\nridge and bring a return to unsettled conditions, especially \nalong the coast by Thursday.",
